; legacy-peer-deps, committed on purpose — this is why CI's npm ci ever
; started working. The lockfile is generated on a machine whose user-level
; ~/.npmrc sets this flag, so the lock deliberately omits auto-installed
; peers: @lobehub/icons hard-declares @lobehub/ui + antd as peers, but this
; site only imports icon components and has no use for either (antd alone
; is a massive tree). Without this file, a stock npm resolves those peers,
; decides the committed lock is incomplete, and `npm ci` fails with EUSAGE
; ("Missing: @lobehub/ui... from lock file") — which is exactly what every
; CI run did until this file existed. Keeping the flag in the repo makes
; every environment (CI, Railway, other machines) resolve dependencies the
; same way the lockfile was written.
legacy-peer-deps=true
